1. Introduction
There is a significant demand for the digitalization of healthcare industries, particularly due to advancements in wireless technologies and electronic equipment. Healthcare 5.0 is the most recent paradigm in healthcare systems, integrating technologies such as
Internet of Things (IoT) with machine learning.1,2 The prevalence of chronic diseases has significantly risen as a result of global economic transformations. The primary threat to human health arises from chronic diseases. Furthermore, if a disease outbreaks, it results in an inflow of patients to hospitals, straining the ability of medical facilities to provide care for all individuals. The worldwide spreading of COVID-19 has exerted pressure on health care facilities globally. Moreover, numerous outpatient departments encounter the issue of congestion in their waiting areas. Patients visiting hospitals present with many diseases, some necessitating urgent medical intervention. The dilemma is further worse when patients requiring emergency care must endure an extended wait in line. The issue is exacerbated in developing nations with inadequately staffed hospitals. A significant number of patients frequently leave without receiving medical care due to hospital overpopulation. The enhancement and maintenance of health is only achievable through the diagnosis and prompt prevention of ailments. Consequently, it is essential to alleviate the strain on healthcare systems while main
taining the quality and standards of healthcare facilities.3 Machine learning and IoT offer potential solutions to alleviate the pressure on healthcare systems. For example, physicians can readily monitor patients’ heart impulses using IoT based healthcare monitoring systems and predict heart diseases with Machine
learning, hence facilitating accurate diagnoses.4 To provide consistent wireless data transfer, numerous wearable devices have been created. The IoT application enables hospitals to monitor the vital signs of patients with chronic diseases. The IoT has ushered in a new era for the healthcare sector, facilitating proactive connections between experts and patients. ML-driven diagnostics can deliver precise and prompt diagnoses, enhancing treatment outcomes. The IoT is anticipated to further proliferate due to developments in sensor technology, networking, and data analytics. With the passage of time, an increased number of medical equipment will be interconnected Through IoT, facilitating real-time monitoring, remote patient management, and the early identification of health concerns. Real-time data transmission enables prompt action, hence diminishing problems and hospital re-admissions. Although transformative changes on ML-IoT healthcare applications are observed in recent times, there are still some shortcomings in the overall synthesis which needs the attention of researchers. This paper offers comprehensive insights and synthesis into the latest technological advancements and role of machine learning and IoT in health care applications. Researchers can benefit from acquiring a thorough awareness of the recent trends of machine learning & IoT. Adopting these technologies and concepts can result in superior patient care, optimized healthcare operations, and innovative research that may transform the future of healthcare delivery. Another contribution is addressing the challenges related to the implementation of machine learning and IoT in healthcare. This knowledge can enable the researchers to link their studies with the urgent demands and issues in healthcare. This paper also highlights future directions and research prospects, as it assists researchers in pinpointing pertinent, effective topics of study that correspond with the dynamic requirements of the healthcare sector. The remainder of the work is structured as follows. Section 2 presents the methodology. Section 3 highlights the State-of-the-Art Developments and case Studies in Healthcare ML-IoT Systems. Section 4 emphasizes on the impact of integrated ML–IoT systems in healthcare applications while addressing its role in improving outcomes in healthcare sectors and key benefits in various applications. Section 5 discusses the result section providing analytical findings derived from the study. Section 6 presents the discussion, outlining the challenges and limitations of this integrated technology, while highlighting future directions and research opportunities for emerging researchers. Section 7 concludes the paper.
2. Materials & method
This review was developed as a narrative review and critical analysis of the literature on machine learning and IoT applications in healthcare. Although it’s a narrative review, it has followed a structured process to ensure transparency of the paper. The strategy details are shared below.
2.1. Literature search strategy
Relevant publications were identified mainly via academic databases such as Scopus, PubMed, Web of Science, ScienceDirect, and Google Scholar, ensuring thorough coverage of recent research. The search incorporates combined keywords and phrases including “machine learning,” “deep learning,” “artificial intelligence,” “Internet of Things,” “IoMT,” “smart healthcare,” “healthcare 5.0,” “remote patient monitoring,” “predictive analytics,” “clinical decision support,” “wearable sensors,” and “personalized medicine.” Boolean operators (AND/OR) were used to find intersections among machine learning techniques, Internet of Things technologies, and healthcare applications. The search was limited to articles published within the past five years to incorporate recent and relevant studies. Furthermore, reference lists from significant review papers, systematic reviews, and reports were examined manually to identify additional relevant sources. The literature review encompassed various study types, such as observational studies, clinical trials, case studies, and computational experiments, to encompass a broad range of applications, benefits, challenges, and future directions of ML-IoT in healthcare.
