3.1. Machine learning algorithms in healthcare
Machine learning algorithms have improved the capabilities of doctors and analysts by finding complex patterns in medical data.
This has made diagnoses more accurate and made it easier to make decisions based on evidence.20–28 As a result, these algorithms also make it easier for predictive models in early disease detection, how it will progress, and how to group patients by risk, which helps with
personalized treatment plans and proactive healthcare management.29–35 Machine learning-based techniques have been applied in large medical centers to increase efficiency in medical imaging and monitoring to identify irregularities in blood samples, organs,
bones, robot-assisted surgeries etc..36–41 ML makes it possible to analyze the massive amounts of heterogeneous data that are produced every day in the healthcare sector originating from multiple sources. The data include medical records, medical images, data from wearable devices, clinical trial data, data from open sources, genomic data, data from health surveys, and cloud platform. By analyzing such data, ML can uncover patterns, predict disease risks, personalize treatment plans, optimize hospital operations, and support timely clinical decision-making. Fig. 2 illustrates the sources of heterogeneous data analyzed by ML. As the healthcare industry expands into the modern technological era, numerous innovations are made. The advancement of the discipline depends on methods and applications based on machine learning, which has led to faster, more accurate, and simpler
diagnosis & prediction processes.42–47 The next subsection outlines the various types of algorithms and their specific applications along with recent trends in healthcare.
3.1.1. Supervised learning for disease diagnosis and prediction
Supervised learning is a machine learning algorithm that uses labeled data to acquire knowledge. The algorithm can be classified into classification and regression algorithms, such as Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, Support Vector Machines (SVM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), and Naive Bayes. These methods are applied when the output variable has a continuous value. Supervised Learning algorithms are used in diagnosis and prediction of different types of diseases including breast cancer, heart diseases, liver disease, thyroid, Alzheimer’s disease, lung cancer, prostate cancer, brain cancer, hepatitis, Covid 19, influenza etc. Breast cancer, the second most diagnosed cancer in women, often relies on mammography, which is only 70 % accurate, leading to potential misdiagnosis. Researchers have applied machine learning for predicting breast cancer by carefully choosing the most effective characteristics, using six supervised learning classifiers: support vector machine, random forest, gradient boosting machine, multilayer perceptron, logistic regression, and K-nearest neighbors (KNN). KNN outperforms all other classifiers on the 20-feature dataset using SVD and achieves a perfect accuracy score of 100 % when employing a PCA with the 15 most significant features,
outperforming other classifiers in terms of accuracy.48 Other researchers utilized a breast cancer dataset comprising 11 features and implemented 8 machine learning classifiers. The results indicated that Logistic Regression attained the highest testing accuracy of 91.67 % without the application of feature selection. Following the implementation of feature selection, classifiers such as LGBM
demonstrated an improvement, achieving an accuracy of 90.74 %.49 Cardiovascular diseases result in approximately 17.9 million fatalities each year, with heart attacks and strokes constituting over 80 % of these deaths. Medical researchers face significant challenges in detecting heart disease accurately. A framework has been
proposed by researchers that integrates contemporary machine learning techniques to improve heart disease prediction.50 Some researchers utilized supervised machine learning classifiers to identify significant features for predicting heart disease. Various classification techniques utilize distinct features within the prediction model. The effectiveness of performance metrics is evaluated in relation to the UCI heart dataset through the application of machine learning techniques, specifically utilizing SMOTE oversampling. The Random Forest method achieved a maximum accuracy of 96.6 %, with sensitivity at 90 %, and both specificity and precision at
