accuracy compared to existing state-of-the-art results for biological datasets across various domains.86 Anomaly detection in medical imaging has proven to be crucial in medical image analysis. The Unsupervised Anomaly Detection (UAD) method aims to differentiate anomalous tissue from normal anatomy by learning from healthy structures. Generative models are frequently employed to generate counterfactual healthy samples from diseased images, facilitating anomaly detection without the
need for pixel-level annotations.87 The use of such models removes the necessity for extensive manual labeling, which can present difficulties. Additionally, it enables the detection of unseen or rare anomaly cases that are underrepresented in the dataset for fully supervised models. However, the inability of these models to reconstruct unhealthy structures results in reconstruction errors that signify anomalies. Nonetheless, a considerable challenge lies in achieving a balance between the precise reconstruction of healthy anatomy and the inadvertent replication of abnormal structures. In recent times, diffusion models have been proven to demonstrate potential for detailed and accurate reconstructions. Some researchers suggested conditioning the denoising process of diffusion models
using supplementary information obtained from a latent representation of the input image.88 Other researchers have proposed an unsupervised anomaly detection model specifically for medical images. The Synomaly noise function was applied to improve the efficacy of anomaly detection. This noise is introduced to healthy training images to replicate the characteristics of actual anomalous tissues following the noising process. A multi-stage diffusion process was proposed for the inference phase. Extensive validation was conducted across various medical imaging modalities, including brain MRI, liver CT, and carotid ultrasound (US) datasets. The findings from the three predominant medical imaging modalities (ultrasound, computed tomography, and magnetic resonance imaging) illustrate the applicability of the proposed method across various contexts. Although unsupervised anomaly detection offers benefits, its effectiveness is significantly influenced by the generative model’s capacity to maintain healthy structures and precisely reconstruct pathological regions as their healthy equivalents. This highlights the necessity for additional investigation into advanced
generative models to improve performance.89 Unsupervised learning has become a critical tool for medical imaging, particularly in tumor segmentation, anomaly detection, and biomedical image clustering, as it mitigates the dependency on costly and time-consuming manual annotations. By minimizing the domain gap between synthetic and real data can enhance unsupervised segmentation performance. Furthermore, leveraging diffusion models or hybrid generative frameworks can ensure more accurate reconstruction of healthy anatomy and precise anomaly detection. Further development of explainable metrics can be carried out to quantify how well models separate normal and pathological tissue. Also, more focus on realistic data synthesis, multi-modal learning, and clinically interpretable generative models to maximize impact. Also, training the model to selectively identify specific types of anomalies while ignoring others can facilitate selective segmentation. These advancements would enable the model to detect and classify anomalies, thereby enhancing its value in clinical decision-making.
