4.2. Key benefits of ML-IoT in healthcare applications
The Internet of Things (IoT) and machine learning (ML) work together to create a strong, data-driven healthcare facility that increases access, enhances clinical results, reduces costs, and facilitates prompt, individualized decisions in a variety of contexts. Continuous physiologic and operational signal acquisition is made possible by the widespread connectivity of medical sensors, wearables, and smart equipment. Machine learning models subsequently convert these high-velocity streams into useful information
for remote monitoring, early diagnosis, treatment selection, and recovery support.250,251 Clinicians may forecast illness risk and recovery windows, customize treatment routes, and act earlier as their understanding of symptoms and trajectories develops. These
abilities rely on interpreting the massive real-time datasets generated by IoT devices.252–254 In order to speed up the identification of mutations, biomarkers, and therapeutic targets that guide precision diagnosis and therapy design, machine learning (ML) first identifies patterns in vast, heterogeneous biomedical corpora, such as literature, registries, hospital
electronic medical records, and sensor streams.255–265 Second, algorithms that automate repetitive processes, minimize data entry and triage errors, and direct limited clinical care to the most vulnerable patients result in cost and resource efficiencies. CNNs, for instance, are experts at quick, precise interpretation in imaging-centric use cases, and IoT-enabled research workflows improve clinical trial operations by reducing data-based errors, unified electronic records, and real-time participant monitoring, all of which reduce costs
and timelines.266–270 For that, Table 3 shows the three different classes of machine learning and their cost effectiveness. By pushing analytics to the edge, systems-level architectures further reduce costs. For example, a recent three-layer remote patient monitoring design (such as sensing to fog/network then cloud/app) quadrupled the number of patients per gateway without compromising data fidelity by using adaptive sampling, filtering, and on-device preprocessing, enhancing patient engagement, safety,
and physician visibility while preventing unnecessary utilization.271
