I. In ML-IoT-based healthcare systems, supervised learning has demonstrated significant efficacy in diagnosing and predicting a range of diseases, such as cancer, cardiovascular disorders, diabetes, kidney disease, liver disease, hepatitis, thyroid conditions, and infectious diseases. The literature studies shows that ensemble models and deep learning have enhanced diagnostic and predictive performance of various diseases. Neural networks, which include CNNs and ANNs, and ensemble methods like Random Forest and XGBoost, continuously enhance latency and achieve high predictive accuracy, typically between 85 % and 95 %. II. Recent advancements, including two-stage synthetic tumor creation and diffusion-based anomaly detection models, exhibit improved segmentation accuracy and the capability to identify unusual or previously unobserved anomalies across many imaging modalities (MRI, CT, US). These methods emphasize that reducing the domain disparity between synthetic and real data, utilizing multi-modal generative frameworks, and integrating clinically interpretable metrics can substantially enhance model reliability, robustness, and applicability in real-world healthcare environments, providing a viable approach for more automated and scalable diagnostic systems. III. In chronic disease management, reinforcement learning frameworks enhance treatment recommendations, medication adherence, and home care support by integrating multimodal patient data and providing automated assistance to patients. In robotic surgery, reinforcement learning automates sub-tasks, enhances surgical precision, alleviates clinician workload, and facilitates semi-autonomous procedures while upholding safety parameters. IV. Furthermore, the review demonstrates that deep learning algorithms substantially surpass conventional methods in processing intricate medical data, including electronic medical records and medical imaging. Models such as LSTM, BioBERT, and transformer-based architectures showed effectiveness in extracting clinical information from noisy and inconsistent records. The integration of structured and unstructured data enhances prognostic accuracy for conditions such as glaucoma and cardiovascular risk. Explainable AI instruments like SHAP and LIME improve transparency, while cultivating clinician trust. V. The review demonstrates that sophisticated machine learning algorithms, such as Random Forest and XGBoost, attain elevated predictive accuracy for equipment malfunctions and patient health hazards, so ensuring safety and economic efficiency. VI. Integration trends demonstrate an increasing utilization of cloud-edge computing for real-time analytics, with lightweight machine learning models implemented on IoT devices enhancing latency and energy efficiency. Although numerous studies indicate favorable therapeutic outcomes, such as improved diagnostics, augmented decision support, individualized treatment, and reduced healthcare expenditures—challenges persist.
6. Discussion
The integration of ML-IoT in healthcare shows considerable potential; however, it encounters critical challenges such as interoperability issues, latency, scalability limitations, data security risks, and ethical issues. Resource-limited IoT devices managing sensitive medical information increase privacy vulnerabilities, whereas cloud and edge systems face challenges due to diverse data types. Black-box models present issues related to transparency and fairness, while variable wireless protocols may influence real-time performance. Future research directions will be examined in detail, focusing on multilayered security, enhanced edge computing, explainable AI, human-in-the-loop approaches, and emerging technologies such as quantum computing and nano-IoT.
6.1. Challenges and limitations
Healthcare systems have advanced tremendously due to the integration of ML and IoT. Internet of Things (IoT) generates a large volume of real-time data from various sensors and devices, necessitating analysis due to their complex characteristics. ML algorithms have demonstrated their crucial significance in the field of IoT. However, these technologies face some notable challenges which must
be addressed when applying ML algorithms in healthcare289
6.1.1. Data security and patient privacy
The healthcare industry has a number of challenges when integrating machine learning (ML) with the Internet of Things (IoT), such as issues with data security, privacy, scalability, and data heterogeneity. Data security and patient privacy are major concerns for MLIoT based healthcare. Data security and privacy issues arise because of the continuous collection, transmission, and storage of sensitive patient information. Also, IoT devices have limited resources and are vulnerable to security breaches. 72 % of hostile operations target sensitive health information, such as genetic and EMR data, hence strong encryption and privacy-preserving measures are
crucial.290–293 Service reliability is also impacted by information security breaches, underscoring the necessity of all-encompassing
protection plans for IoT-enabled devices.294,295
6.1.2. Scalability and data heterogeneity
Scalability poses a considerable challenge in implementing ML-IoT solutions in the healthcare sector. The increasing prevalence of IoT devices, sensors, and patients leads to a significant rise in data generation, resulting in challenges for infrastructure management. Studies indicate that although decentralized learning approaches, such as federated learning and blockchain, effectively preserve
