Nano ML-IoT is a significant future direction for healthcare, integrating nanoscale devices, Internet of Things connectivity, and machine learning. The Internet of Nano Things (IoNT) has advanced considerably in the healthcare sector, providing expedited and
more convenient methods for diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment.315,316 Healthcare functions like electromagnetic detection,
transmission, and actuation are improved by combining opto-technology with ML-IoT-based systems.317 By linking medical equipment
and facilitating remote health data sharing, this integration makes smart healthcare possible.318 Contactless feature extraction and
enhanced data analysis are made possible by sophisticated signal processing techniques.319 Effective data aggregation requires
addressing performance degradation in dynamic and diverse situations.320–322 Through expert knowledge and ML-based techniques, architectures such as IoT-HDHM-ML employ networks of wearable nodes to continuously monitor numerous patient parameters in real
time, hence boosting healthcare efficiency.323 Furthermore, Explainable AI (XAI) can provide interpretable and transparent predictions, helping clinicians trust and adopt ML-IoT systems in critical decision-making processes, while Quantum Computing offers the potential to accelerate ML model training and support complex data analysis in genomics and medical imaging, further enhancing healthcare applications. Future studies should concentrate on creating scalable, resilient opto-ML-IoT infrastructures that can function in dynamic, diverse contexts.
6.2.3. Involvement of stakeholders
The involvement of Stakeholders including professionals in the fields of engineering and health sciences as well as patients is crucial to make Healthcare 5.0 successful. Stakeholders in healthcare, also including patients, providers, and insurers, maintain a
single ledger that encodes pre-authorization requirements, ensuring transparency and accountability.324 Patients must be involved in
the design process and the ML-IoT technologies must be patient-centered.325 Additionally, while designing ML-IoT-based digital
healthcare systems, receiving feedback from users of such systems and considering individual requirements are also essential.326 Moreover, in the healthcare system, Blockchain provides additional benefits such as real-time calculations through the automation of information distribution and it typically employs cryptographic-based techniques (such hash functions, asymmetric encryption, and
digital signatures) across different system stakeholders to help maintain trust throughout interactions.327
6.2.4. Standardization of protocols
To solve interoperability issues in healthcare IoT systems, standardization and synchronization are crucial.328 Data transmission between new machine learning (ML)-based healthcare technologies and current electronic health records is made easier by ensuring that data formats are uniform and analyzable. To facilitate the incorporation of cutting-edge healthcare solutions, policymakers should give priority to data compatibility, sharing, and interoperability. The creation of protocols and universal data standards for ML-IoT healthcare systems should be the main goal of future research. Integration can be improved by looking at automated mapping and translation solutions between heterogeneous data sources. Policy-driven frameworks that direct the adoption of new technologies while preserving compliance, compatibility, and safe data transmission across various healthcare systems could also be the subject of future research.
6.2.5. Human-AI collaboration in healthcare
In the healthcare industry, human-AI collaboration refers to the use of AI systems in conjunction with people as partners to address difficult issues, such as supporting clinical decision support systems (CDSS) in the diagnosis of different diseases including can
cer.329,330 Human-AI collaboration facilitates augmented decision making. Augmented decision-making utilizes AI insights to assist clinicians by providing evidence-based recommendations, thereby enhancing accuracy and minimizing diagnostic errors. Collaboration between doctors and AI, grounded in ethical and transparent practices, guarantees that human oversight, explainability, and fairness are integral to patient care decisions. Medical professionals can choose the best machine learning methods utilizing human-in-the-loop ensemble learning when human experience is incorporated into ML-IoT systems. This improves prediction accuracy
and model performance.331–333 Despite obstacles including bias in CDSS, skepticism about AI, and adoption issues, this partnership can
lessen the scarcity of qualified healthcare workers, enhance the quality of care, and lighten the workload of clinicians.334,335 Future studies should examine methods for teaching healthcare workers how to collaborate with AI and include these techniques into healthcare education curricula. Wider adoption will be supported by research into ways to improve the usability, transparency,
